# Files that must never reach the registry.
#
# This module had no .moonignore at all until 0.3.4, so nothing was excluded --
# and `moon package --list` showed the consequence. The published 0.3.4 tarball
# shipped:
#
#   _qa/bigtree2.log    261966 bytes of scan output naming local absolute paths
#   _qa/bigtree.log       286 bytes, same
#   _qa/commitmsg.txt    2499 bytes, a scratch commit message
#   cvc5wrap.ps1           637 bytes, a machine-local shim hardcoding
#                           C:\Users\<account>\.moon\bin\cvc5.exe
#
# None of those are credentials, but none belong in a public package either. The
# build dir (_build) is already excluded by the toolchain itself; everything
# below had to be named explicitly.
#
# THE FILE NAME IS NOT A CHOICE, AND IT IS NOT `.mooncakesignore`.
#
# The toolchain's packaging rule (release note 2026-09-21, "Added support for
# .moonignore files"): "Packaging now follows conventional ignore-file rules: the
# .moonignore in a folder -- or, if absent, .gitignore -- is used as the ignore
# file. By default, files and folders starting with `.` are ignored (this rule can
# be overridden via the ignore file), as is the _build folder (not overridable).
# The `exclude` and `include` fields in moon.mod will be deprecated."
#
# So the packager reads THIS file. It is not `.mooncakesignore` -- `moon publish
# --help` and `moon package --help` expose no ignore flag, the release notes and
# the packaging docs name only `.moonignore`, and a file the packager never reads
# is a comment.
#
# And it is NOT inherited from `.gitignore`: `.moonignore` REPLACES `.gitignore`
# as the ignore file. `moon package` does not read `.gitignore`, so every rule
# that matters has to appear in BOTH files, which is why the `_verify` rules
# below repeat the ones already in `.gitignore`.
#
# WHAT IS EXCLUDED HERE, and what is not, was measured rather than guessed:
#
#   `moon package --list` on a dirty working tree   811 entries
#   the published 0.4.33 tree                        413 files
#
# The difference is that CI checks out CLEAN, so the ~400 untracked scratch files
# never reach the registry -- and the only TRACKED junk that did reach it was
# FOUR `COMMIT_*.md` files, which shipped in every published version up to and
# including 0.4.33. Those are the first rule below. The rest is defence in
# depth: the release policy says every publish goes through CI, and a rule that
# only holds because a checkout happens to be clean is a rule that holds by
# accident.

# Mooncakes credentials. `moon publish` would upload a live token otherwise --
# verified, `moon package --list` listed this file before the rule existed.
# The token belongs in ~/.moon/credentials.json, and the copy CI needs belongs
# in Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions as MOONCAKES_RIANTR_TOKEN.
credentials.json
*.credentials.json

# Measurement scratch: per-file scan listings and working commit messages.
# Evidence, not source -- the numbers they contain are already written up in
# EXTENSIONS.md. Git ignores this directory; moon package does not read
# .gitignore, so it has to be named here too.
_qa/

# Machine-local formal-verification shims (same reasoning as .gitignore).
.why3.conf
cvc5wrap.ps1

# Scratch commit messages. 23 of them at the repository root, of which FOUR ARE
# TRACKED and therefore reached the registry in every published version up to
# and including 0.4.33 (MEASURED against the published 0.4.33 tree). Nothing
# links to them and nothing reads them -- the release record is CHANGELOG.md
# and _verify/NARRATIVE.md, both of which ship.
COMMIT_*.md

# Scan artifacts. The name is provenance for a scan of SOME OTHER project;
# shipping twenty-four of this project's own scans is noise, and each one is a
# megabyte of somebody else's source quoted back.
*.mmd

# A downloaded archive, and a stray file written by a shell redirect that lost
# its target. `$null` is a literal name, not a variable.
pdf2md.zip
$null

# `_verify` scratch. One rule, because every scratch file in there is named with
# a leading underscore -- the tracked exception is re-included on the next line.
#
# The gates themselves are NOT excluded, and deliberately so: `_verify/mutate_*.ps1`
# (79 of them), `_verify/settle_*.ps1` and `_verify/NARRATIVE.md` are the
# EVIDENCE for every number this project publishes, the README names
# `_verify/check_gate_roster.ps1`, and the published-package self-scan reads the
# shipped tree. The scratch is the working copy, not the evidence.
_verify/_*
!_verify/_split_release_narrative.ps1

# Working copies of release runs and of the scratch commit messages, which are
# NOT underscore-prefixed and so are not covered by the rule above.
_verify/release_*.txt
_verify/commitmsg*.txt

# The per-file scan dumps `remeasure.ps1` writes. Also not underscore-prefixed.
_verify/remeasure_out/

# One file that must never ship, named individually because a shape rule would
# have to exclude real test files too.
#
# `src/lexer/lexer_0488_test.mbt` is SUPERSEDED -- its rows live in
# `src/parser/parser_0488_test.mbt`, because a test file lives in the package it
# tests and `src/lexer` cannot see `@parser` (Error 4020). What is left is a
# comment-only stub, and its own header says "It is NOT tracked and must not be
# committed". It is not tracked. But `moon package` reads the WORKING TREE and
# not git, so a LOCAL publish would ship a file the repository itself declares
# un-committable. The published tree is built from a clean checkout, so this
# only ever bit a local publish -- which is why it is named here rather than
# left to the checkout being clean.
src/lexer/lexer_0488_test.mbt
